              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "OK, so I want to explain the concept of food chains and food webs. These are fundamental ideas in ecology that help us understand how energy moves through ecosystems.\nA food chain is a simple model showing how energy passes from one organism to another. It starts with producers, which are usually plants. Plants capture energy from the sun through photosynthesis. Then primary consumers, like rabbits or insects, eat the plants. Secondary consumers, such as small birds or frogs, eat the primary consumers. And at the top, you might have tertiary consumers, like hawks or foxes, eating the secondary consumers.\nNow, in reality, ecosystems are more complicated than a simple chain. Most animals eat more than one type of food, and most organisms are eaten by more than one predator. So scientists use the term food web to describe these interconnected relationships. A food web is basically many food chains linked together.\nWhy does this matter? Well, if one species in a food web disappears, it affects many others. For example, if a disease kills most of the rabbits in an area, the foxes that eat rabbits will struggle, but so will the plants that rabbits used to keep in check. Everything is connected. Understanding food webs helps us predict how changes in one population might ripple through an entire ecosystem."
