              "script": "Alright, today I want to compare two different strategies animals use to regulate their body temperature—being warm-blooded versus cold-blooded. These terms are a bit simplified, but they help us understand some fundamental differences.\nWarm-blooded animals, which include mammals and birds, generate their own body heat internally. Their metabolism is constantly working to maintain a stable internal temperature regardless of the environment. A human body stays around 37 degrees Celsius whether you’re in a hot desert or a cold mountain. This gives warm-blooded animals a significant advantage—they can remain active in a wide range of conditions.\nCold-blooded animals, like reptiles, fish, and insects, work differently. They rely on external sources to regulate their temperature. A lizard, for instance, will bask in the sun to warm up and seek shade when it gets too hot. Their body temperature changes with their surroundings.\nNow, each strategy has trade-offs. Warm-blooded animals can be active anytime, but they need to eat a lot more food to fuel their metabolism. A mouse might need to eat half its body weight daily. Cold-blooded animals need much less food, but their activity is limited by environmental conditions. On cold days, they become sluggish and slow.\nNeither approach is superior—they’re just different solutions to the same problem of surviving in various environments. Next, we will examine how some animals can actually switch between these strategies."
