              "script": "OK, so let’s talk about something you encounter every day but might not think much about—the states of matter. Most of you know there are three main states: solid, liquid, and gas. But do you understand what’s actually happening at the molecular level?\nIn a solid, molecules are packed tightly together in fixed positions. They vibrate but don’t move around freely. This is why solids maintain their shape—put a rock on a table, and it stays exactly where you put it. The molecules are locked in place.\nLiquids are different. The molecules are still close together, but they can slide past one another. This is why liquids take the shape of their container. Pour water into a glass, and it conforms to the glass’s shape. The molecules have more freedom to move but are still attracted to each other.\nIn gases, molecules have the most energy and freedom. They spread out to fill whatever space is available and move rapidly in all directions. The molecules are far apart and barely interact with each other. This is why gases can be compressed easily—there’s lots of empty space between molecules.\nWhat determines which state matter is in? Temperature and pressure. Add heat energy, and molecules move faster. Eventually, a solid becomes liquid, and a liquid becomes gas. Remove heat, and the opposite happens. It’s all about how much energy the molecules have."
